<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madame de Pompadour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1764">1764</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jeanne Antoinette Poisson, Marquise de Pompadour</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeanne Antoinette Poisson, Marquise de Pompadour, was a member of the French court who was the official chief mistress of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> from 1745 to 1751 and remained influential as court favourite until her death. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1721, she moved in the salons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and the king gave her the marquisate of Pompadour in 1745; she was made a duchess in 1752 and lady-in-waiting to the queen in 1756. She shaped appointments and domestic and foreign policy, patronised the philosophes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, and bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> factory at Sèvres in 1759. She died of tuberculosis in 1764 at the age of 42.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madame-de-pompadour.jpg" alt="Madame de Pompadour" width="330" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Boucher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madame_de_Pompadour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jeanne Antoinette Poisson was the daughter of François Poisson, a steward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> brothers who financed the French state. It was suspected that her biological father was a financier or the tax collector Charles François Paul Le Normant de Tournehem, who became her guardian and paid for an expensive education, with tutors including the dramatist Crébillon and the singer Jélyotte. At twenty she married his nephew Charles Guillaume Le Normant d'Étiolles. She attended Paris salons, held her own at Étiolles, and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and Helvétius there. In 1744 she contrived to be seen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> while he hunted, and at a masked ball at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 25 February 1745 he declared his interest in her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By March 1745 she was the king's mistress. The king bought her the marquisate of Pompadour in June, and she was presented at court on 14 September. She won the favour of Queen Marie Leszczyńska, became a duchess in 1752 and lady-in-waiting to the queen in 1756. She acted in effect as a prime minister, handling appointments, favours and dismissals, and in 1755 the Austrian diplomat Kaunitz approached her during the talks leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, which allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Later she brought the duc de Choiseul into office. France was diminished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, and she was widely blamed. Around 1750 her sexual relationship with the king ended, and she remained his confidante.</p><p>Libels called poissonnades, a pun on her name, attacked her. Historians stress that the Parc-aux-Cerfs, a house at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> where the king later met young women, was not the harem of legend, and that she only accepted it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Pompadour was a major patron of architecture, painting and the decorative arts, supporting Boucher, Nattier and the engraver Jacques Guay, who taught her to engrave gems. In 1759 she bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> factory at Sèvres. She protected the physician and economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-quesnay">François Quesnay</a> and defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and d'Alembert against those who wished to suppress it. She made 52 prints after Guay's engraved stones and acted in plays at her private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>. She died of tuberculosis on 15 April 1764, aged 42.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madame de Pompadour” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noblewomen and rich bourgeois women presided over the salons of Paris, meeting places of the Enlightenment, and a few won fame in their own right: Émilie du Châtelet translated Newton’s Principia into French, and Jeanne-Antoinette Poisson, a commoner, became the <b>Marquise de Pompadour</b> and Louis XV’s adviser.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Versailles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Madame de Pompadour"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748">What was here in 1748? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
